#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110313 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "c02ce0b7-d660-5d31-8147-303a9e6f806d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1223" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x) = (0.2)^x?$\n\nI. The domain is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and the range is $y\\gt 0.$\n\nII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $\\left( 1,0.2\\right).$\n\nIII. $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x) = (0.2)^x$ is defined for all input values $x,$ and, the set of possible outputs consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(1) = (0.2)^1 = 0.2 .\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ decreases, the function value gets larger and larger, approaching *positive* infinity. Therefore $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110314 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46b4837f-6ba5-5812-8dc7-4671fcd6b20d" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Scaled Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/id #uuid "90054a4d-adcf-56b8-ab73-86048bc0f4d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1224" :question/problem "Plot the graph of ![](images/ee/ee7c536e7564d206978b4714c551e52f72bcd604881d7a9c64d2ca352432d060.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of ![](images/05/05e47fd146d7d132ae3688982e5f30c0f592790360a98b13f1d13b66ae53c645.svg)\n\n![](images/01/01d329ac60c1a70c38fc89d8d679879f8e584cd4eb9d55412a3c43d6d43c8129.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of ![](images/92/92a113026443c67e5e8a7680835f0ae448b4a2c8ba773421e478cafe1c638f6f.svg) The ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept becomes ![](images/90/900a9e2e74d5210f297c674edf09e4f42d338f866eaa61db18ac8c4047ebfbd8.svg) and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/e6/e6858bdc1668b19f76369796febbe89208bc98a6f553737db87421d4920f35c3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110315 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34645eb8-3624-5ff3-817d-28f8d0cee4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Scaling Constant From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "055bdf27-d354-57d7-96a6-de3300053035" :question/math-academy-id "e-1225" :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=k\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^x.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n![](images/24/247e8b4712be05f38cfba373d930c5c8ee65ab64c9619e4ee74ec488fbd0c61d.png)" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k\\left(\\dfrac25\\right)^x$ we take the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac 25\\right)^x$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,1.5)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=1.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110370 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41d2e6d0-bfad-5fe0-a055-aaa9ed9ae31c" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of an Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/id #uuid "edf79f97-9325-55a0-a78b-133bba524177" :question/math-academy-id "e-1803" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-1} =4.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $\\left( -1, 4\\right).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/4c/4cbb5007cf380afe964bea8b5970e4a37924f38401ec24cb856b02c0510676eb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34645eb8-3624-5ff3-817d-28f8d0cee4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Scaling Constant From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc34e3d-7f5a-5410-9872-37c1fdc40860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282086} {:answer/id #uuid "4f619c4c-928e-5b2c-a2f6-9650ca210e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282370} {:answer/id #uuid "726c1b73-a586-5d60-b744-3f50a3ac17d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282669} {:answer/id #uuid "c39485e4-e8d5-5bd1-a275-66e3b3650f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283393} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd21f5f-8b88-5095-9dca-4fe1f18d7abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c39485e4-e8d5-5bd1-a275-66e3b3650f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283393} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0bec54-298c-5799-a502-9235e4f8885b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f5ee281-743c-52e5-8ba6-b17dfb13fd85" :question/math-academy-id "q-167338" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec6deda5b2400258af6ad455cdfa0072deb754391feb4d916082568c248d95ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,7)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46b4837f-6ba5-5812-8dc7-4671fcd6b20d" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Scaled Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d33b258-c127-5e48-89c1-172347927c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1abbfb9c3aeaaeed74af4948b0c645384d6f20c04c23d435224b855cf1192e6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507627} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2779e9-a749-57a4-aa3d-9cd1888dce16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1e5ea7ddfefb6f1ef81e75c123c528327618ca73bc01df764d2cf3e9564835e1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507657} {:answer/id #uuid "43aa1de5-a93e-57fb-ba78-684b4247686a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb5ac2100e6106e62b930a9a0cb94d8ce315dc41c4e15a40c5e02f5c5cacfa82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507702} {:answer/id #uuid "e23c2923-cf2d-5e5e-9c53-dfb0399ef7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e7e697fe18c032e596590c68a04f0b01893b44ade18c6fcc732a79bf12ebcf4a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507902} {:answer/id #uuid "fe180236-0e5b-5dc5-967b-2a36380c113d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "im