 #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efa980a6-6790-54ec-92bb-d5c383a444ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-28968" :question/problem "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y=(0.6)^{-1}=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,\\frac{5}{3}).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/0c/0c5bf6531bf352da158771f92eaab5a3974ccd623d1bf29dbe3ecba287f61d5d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f8b571-b7ad-5efc-bfb0-6e49db6e3e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450366} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8be265-db8c-50ff-9d68-fe21746b4787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d8be265-db8c-50ff-9d68-fe21746b4787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450367} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad4dbfc-bcc0-5e6d-b6d4-e46c856c03d8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451137} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f3a4f6-e5c9-5b68-a65e-10a9475ced99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450368} {:answer/id #uuid "a76ed30d-31c8-5203-8dd8-5ec99bf68b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28f3a4f6-e5c9-5b68-a65e-10a9475ced99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450368} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb6c276-d8fd-5138-9a90-6c09789f740d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451138} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7ba5258-5378-53a5-88db-6afd66703ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450370} {:answer/id #uuid "fc711f4a-7d29-5e54-b376-a1498498fbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc711f4a-7d29-5e54-b376-a1498498fbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450371} :answer-field/id #uuid "45d53e54-8c42-56fe-87b8-111b8be08363" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c7808ae-1f8f-5217-b026-8ee08973156b" :question/math-academy-id "q-28981" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.08)^{x}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Its range is $y>0$\n\n- **II.** It is an increasing function\n\n- **III.** $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(0.08)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4e3b5119c92d02bbceb786fefd238182c7c84ca0f4abc85a19f2df86a6ba267.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(0.08)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41d2e6d0-bfad-5fe0-a055-aaa9ed9ae31c" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of an Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e10503b-edb6-5b90-9853-cb5f2c3ff972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507630} {:answer/id #uuid "2494f4ae-d21d-5b05-9615-a60fc623cee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bc226e70ed1cd19eca5cca6d82d6e72d4fa1ccd6f243aa7feca9134c11b93ba1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507641} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa46daf-b83a-59a7-a072-244a33736204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b843d7fa9628622fc74ea9989fddb8b459952369c6bd6b4e9cecd754cb4acf0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507660} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e41aa6-94d0-5182-b006-2e6cf57099a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/286752cfbadff12d52d940b193f37cf4f4fade6da05f239f350905ce128ebabf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507889} {:answer/id #uuid "ff96ccd6-24f2-5ea1-8f70-ebd65b0d2c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48a219b02ff28ff978dea320428b3afbc6f6cae3b9265b2c21c471ee751e542f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e10503b-edb6-5b90-9853-cb5f2c3ff972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507630} :answer-field/id #uuid "f06659c0-7b4f-5aa8-8396-f34d621a1627" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8c01898-d5f3-593c-be7d-e90b6733c0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-29397" :question/problem "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{-1}=5.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,5).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069bd935-a3e1-5a4b-b5b2-e9d5897750e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507609} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb16e5f-ba42-5bd9-90eb-f56bf17bbb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507715} {:answer/id #uuid "803213b4-62ce-516b-810e-b7062290768e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507790} {:answer/id #uuid "9b850d8c-4056-5cb7-8709-ad0f4ffda5d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507815} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fdd93e-817a-5ad8-aca9-7e39b7a8f224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "069bd935-a3e1-5a4b-b5b2-e9d5897750e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507609} :answer-field/id #uuid "d60905be-5479-5411-b5d1-1ac224418f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94e841e5-3216-5691-bf5f-488fa6567b77" :question/math-academy-id "q-47731" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its range is $y>\\frac{4}{5}$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/82/8250b7e30d7960f7c06d0e254e8bad97546e959be1ad3128eb491800b6e91533.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers, so the range is  $y>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ee41f75-75e8-560d-a0d8-a810a881a517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507693} {:answer/id #uuid "70f163bc-40df-5709-9347-836972090890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507762} {:answer/id #uuid "b4db0f16-b037-5c85-9197-78dadf2c5e6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507846} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ed03b7-54fd-5290-92c9-dacae031c94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507849} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a08e1c-63f5-575a-8ab3-1f91c228a8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5ed03b7-54fd-5290-92c9-dacae031c94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507849} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aba8ef7-d5cb-5b87-8948-2023f65a58ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fbf8c06-659f-590c-beea-a6431f0cb137" :question/math-academy-id "q-47746" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(-1,4).$\n\nIII. It's an increasing function" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/5d/5d97ca13c1289558c6bb8e8ce50fb1ef5745f54acf27346a32d6451b1bb7f9bb.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x,$ so the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(-1)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-1}=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c4946f-7cfd-5eea-9b2a-3c68b8c04bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451423} {:answer/id #uuid "631df741-fcdf-5ee9-b554-6ce847f8201f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631df741-fcdf-5ee9-b554-6ce847f8201f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451424} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae7393b-6d61-58de-92a9-e49a25b03a56" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452559} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec9a444e-8f67-59aa-984d-88ba3aa4a432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451425} {:answer/id #uuid "c1829ff3-c9af-5728-b060-4f94a8c1209f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1829ff3-c9af-5728-b060-4f94a8c1209f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451426} :answer-field/id #uuid "fac9daa5-4d61-5666-831a-625724e0a0cc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452560} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59f4be26-745f-500a-8014-e08a471c0a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451427} {:answer/id #uuid "d66a88ac-8487-5227-85df-df58a16cb930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59f4be26-745f-500a-8014-e08a471c0a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451427} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a523b7-faa6-5772-a250-4264622204cb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f25b7dd1-f51b-52ea-a0c4-08f9ed823c0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47747" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Its domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty $\n- **II.** It's a decreasing function\n- **III.** $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/28/288adf3d1da5cf4bb6e2530318aeb083b69d43639de7704f9df15672be477d0a.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34645eb8-3624-5ff3-817d-28f8d0cee4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Scaling Constant From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2f1c83-8e64-5c5d-a7c8-61fec0e2b212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336438} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae8a4ab-e8dd-5c9e-8034-034f85b09221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336858} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b1b13-e0bb-5ca4-9acc-1057a08b5578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337167} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d09915-1432-5fb6-8119-db2c86b226d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337770} {:answer/id #uuid "ded27ff2-fbdc-54ae-a21b-ad7fdc344c0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ae8a4ab-e8dd-5c9e-8034-034f85b09221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336858} :answer-field/id #uuid "303db511-661b-5dcd-ac91-ae15b1d84c40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bd82a27-9501-5619-90c8-a56e5e1d1b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-7246" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f960cca1134d756cded381a2236dd427efb928b1081254173e4c6903b6de150e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x},$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,1.5)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=1.5.$"}]}
